## 1. Scope

This reference covers composite tubes used in dropout and distribution cutouts. These tubes work outdoors for years, interrupt repeatedly, and are handled by crews with hot sticks, which is a combination of requirements that does not apply to a fuse inside an enclosure.

## 2. Construction

- Composite body with a gas generating inner element and a structural outer wall.
- Buttons, trunnions and contact hardware fitted to the tube as part of the cutout assembly.
- Outdoor grade materials for UV and weather exposure.
- Length and diameter matched to the cutout frame and the voltage class.

## 3. Typical specification range

| Parameter | Typical value | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Voltage classes | Distribution cutout classes up to 36 kV | Match to the cutout frame and the system voltage. |
| Duty | Repeated interruption of distribution fault currents | Established by type test on the cutout assembly. |
| Environment | Outdoor, UV exposed, all weather | Weathering resistance is a design requirement, not a finish. |
| Hardware fit | Trunnions, buttons and contacts to the cutout design | Hardware interface tolerances decide whether the tube drops out cleanly. |

Values above describe what is commonly specified for this component class. Confirm the exact figure against the datasheet for the grade being purchased before design release.

## 4. Selection and sizing

- The cutout frame and voltage class the tube has to fit, including the hardware interface.
- The fault level the cutout is expected to clear repeatedly.
- Weather exposure at the site, particularly UV intensity and humidity.
- Whether the tube is supplied bare or with hardware fitted.
- Whether the requirement is a replacement tube or a tube for a new cutout design.

## 5. Standards commonly specified

- IEC 60282-2, high voltage fuses for expulsion fuse designs.
- IEEE C37.41, design tests for high voltage fuses.
- IEEE C37.42, distribution cutouts and fuse links.
- ASTM G154, UV exposure practice for non-metallic materials.
- IEC 60587, tracking and erosion under severe conditions.

Standards are listed as those normally cited for this component class. They describe the requirement, not a claim of certification. Test evidence applicable to a specific order is confirmed per purchase order.

## 6. Handling and installation

- Fit and handle with the tooling the crew actually uses, since the tube is installed from a hot stick.
- Check that the tube drops cleanly before commissioning. A tube that hangs up does not clear the circuit as designed.
- Protect the bore during storage and transport.
- Confirm hardware is fitted to the specified torque so the drop-out mechanism works.

## 7. Failure modes and mitigation

| Failure mode | Likely cause | Mitigation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Tube does not drop out after operation | Hardware interface fouling, or the tube partially seized in the frame | Verify the drop test on the assembly before commissioning and keep the interface clean and dry. |
| Arc ablation after repeated interruption | Tube duty exceeded, or a liner not matched to the fault level | Confirm the tube against the cutout duty and the type test evidence. |
| Moisture uptake and swelling | Composite left unsealed in humid storage | Store sealed and assemble from dry stock. |
| Mechanical damage from hot stick handling | Crew handling load above what the wall accepts at the contact points | Confirm the tube wall against the hardware clamping arrangement. |

## 8. Typical applications

- Distribution cutouts on overhead lines.
- Dropout fuse assemblies in utility networks.
- Projects in humid and high UV regions where weather resistance drives material choice.
- Utility hardware manufacturers and grid distributors maintaining existing cutout populations.

